<ArticleLayout slug="instagram-reels-stories-specs" ctaLabel="Create your account" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Instagram's own help pages on Reels and Stories don't print 1080 by 1920. They print a ratio range, a quality floor and a length. The only pixel sizes and the only safe zone Meta publishes sit on its ads pages, and they govern ads.</p>

  <p>Reviewed October 8, 2026.</p>

  <h2>What is the Instagram Reel size in 2026?</h2>

  <p>Shoot and export 9:16. Instagram accepts Reels anywhere from 1.91:1 to 9:16, at 720 pixels or more and 30 frames per second, and prints no pixel size for an organic post. Meta's ads guide recommends 1440 by 2560 for Reels ads. hubStudio's Video editor writes a Reel at 1080 by 1920, which clears every size floor on this page.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Instagram's help page on Reel size accepts any aspect ratio between 1.91:1 and 9:16, asks for a minimum frame rate of 30 frames per second and a minimum resolution of 720 pixels, and prints no recommended pixel size.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Instagram Help Center, "Reel size &amp; aspect ratios on Instagram," read October 8, 2026, full page text. https://help.instagram.com/1038071743007909</p>

  <p>Everything below was read on Instagram's and Meta's own pages on October 8, 2026: the Instagram Help Center, the Meta Business Help Center, the Meta Ads Guide and Instagram's developer reference. Where Meta publishes nothing on a point, the table says so rather than filling the gap.</p>

