  <p>Length is where most spec sheets slip. There are five numbers, and each one governs a different way of posting.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Reels can be recorded and edited up to 20 minutes, and Reels over 3 minutes won't be recommended to new audiences. To be boosted, a Reel must run 90 seconds or less in a full-screen 9:16 frame.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Instagram Help Center, "Record a reel on Instagram" and "Boost an Instagram Reel," read October 8, 2026, full page text. https://help.instagram.com/2720958398006062</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Through the API, a Reel runs 3 seconds to 15 minutes and 300MB at most, and a Story video runs 3 to 60 seconds and 100MB at most, in MP4 or MOV with H.264 or HEVC, 23 to 60 frames per second.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Instagram developer reference, "IG User Media," read October 8, 2026, specification sections. https://developers.facebook.com/docs/instagram-platform/instagram-graph-api/reference/ig-user/media</p>

  <p>Read together: 20 minutes is what the app lets you record. Fifteen minutes caps an API upload, and an ad. Past 3 minutes a Reel isn't recommended to new audiences, so that's the line to watch if the goal is reach. Ninety seconds only matters if you plan to boost.</p>

  <p>Stories split the other way.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Story video up to 60 seconds plays as one clip, and longer videos are broken up into multiple clips.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Instagram Help Center, "Share a photo or video to your Instagram story," iPhone app help, read October 8, 2026, full page text. https://help.instagram.com/1257341144298972</p>

  <p>A Stories ad can run an hour, though Meta says a video ad of 16 seconds or more may be shown as one, two or three cards, tailored to each viewer.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta's ads guide sets Stories video ads at 9:16 and 1440 by 2560 pixels, 1 second to 60 minutes and up to 4GB. A separate Meta help article recommends 1080 by 1920 for Stories ads, with 600 by 1067 as the minimum.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Ads Guide, Awareness video ad specs on Instagram Stories, and Meta Business Help Center, "Design requirements for Instagram Stories ads," both read October 8, 2026. https://www.facebook.com/business/ads-guide/update/video/instagram-story</p>

  <p>That's two recommended sizes for one placement, from one company. Both are 9:16, so either fills the screen.</p>

  <h2>What is the Instagram Reels safe zone?</h2>

  <p>Meta publishes one safe zone, and it is written for ads. Instagram's Help Center prints none for an organic Reel or Story.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta asks advertisers to consider leaving at least 14 percent of the top, 35 percent of the bottom and 6 percent of each side of a Reels ad free of text, logos and other important elements, so they are not cropped, covered by the profile icon or call to action, or pushed against the edge of a screen taller than 9:16. The Stories ad pages give the same three figures.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Ads Guide, Awareness video ad specs on Instagram Reels and on Instagram Stories, read October 8, 2026, figures as printed on each page. https://www.facebook.com/business/ads-guide/update/video/instagram-reels</p>

  <p>Meta's help article on text overlays adds two rules, and the second one explains why the margins exist at all.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Reels ad that carries a disclaimer should leave the bottom 40 percent free of text, logos and other key elements. On a screen taller than 9:16, Meta may zoom the creative to fill it, cropping what sits outside the safe zone, or show it at 9:16 on a black background.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Business Help Center, "About text overlays and the safe zone for ads in Stories and Reels," read October 8, 2026, full article text. https://www.facebook.com/business/help/980593475366490</p>

