  <h2>Questions about Instagram Reels and Stories specs</h2>

  <h3>What size should an Instagram Reel be in 2026?</h3>

  <p>Make it 9:16. Instagram accepts Reels from 1.91:1 to 9:16 at 720 pixels or more and 30 frames per second, and its Help Center names no pixel size. For ads, Meta's ads guide recommends 1440 by 2560. A 1080 by 1920 export clears every floor Instagram and Meta print, and so does 1440 by 2560.</p>

  <h3>How long can an Instagram Reel be?</h3>

  <p>It depends on how you post. In the app you can record and edit up to 20 minutes. Through the API, a Reel runs 3 seconds to 15 minutes. A Reels ad runs up to 15 minutes, and a boosted Reel must be 90 seconds or less. Instagram says Reels over 3 minutes won't be recommended to new audiences.</p>

  <h3>How long can an Instagram Story video be?</h3>

  <p>On Instagram's help page, a Story video up to 60 seconds plays as one clip, and longer videos are split into several clips. Through the API, a Story video runs 3 to 60 seconds and 100MB at most. A Stories ad can run 1 second to 60 minutes, split into cards from 16 seconds.</p>

  <h3>What is the Instagram Reels safe zone?</h3>

  <p>Meta's figure is for ads: leave at least 14 percent of the top, 35 percent of the bottom and 6 percent of each side free of text and logos. On a 1080 by 1920 frame that is about 269 pixels at the top, 672 at the bottom and 65 on each side, by our arithmetic. Reels ads with a disclaimer keep the bottom 40 percent clear.</p>

  <h3>Does Instagram publish a safe zone for organic Reels?</h3>

  <p>No. The Instagram Help Center pages on Reels and Stories print no safe zone, in percent or in pixels. The only figure Meta publishes sits on its ads pages. Using it for organic Reels is a sound habit, but any organic pixel inset you see online is someone else's measurement, not Instagram's rule.</p>

  <h3>What size is an Instagram Reel cover?</h3>

  <p>Instagram's Help Center recommends 420 by 654 pixels, a 1:1.55 ratio, and says a cover can't be changed after upload. Meta's developer reference recommends 9:16, crops other shapes to their middle 9:16 rectangle, and shows the middle square when the Reel is shared to feed. Keep the subject centered.</p>

  <h3>Why can't I boost my Reel?</h3>

  <p>Instagram's help pages list the reasons: the Reel runs over 90 seconds, isn't full-screen 9:16, uses licensed music, was already shared to Facebook, or carries face or camera effects, GIFs, product tags or tappable elements. A video whose resolution is too low is refused as well.</p>
